<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/foundational/training-load-adaptation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Training doesn't make you fitter. <strong>Training plus recovery</strong> makes you fitter —
    the exercise is only the stimulus. A hard session temporarily depresses your capacity
    (fatigue), then the body over-rebuilds above baseline — <strong>supercompensation</strong> —
    before drifting back if nothing further happens. The whole of programming (when to train
    hard, when to rest, when to test) is the art of timing the next session onto that peak.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one session, plotted — the dip is fatigue; the bump above baseline is adaptation; the crossings mark the window" widget="plotter" config={superCfg} />

  <p>
    The circled points are where the green curve crosses baseline: about <strong>1–2 days of
    fatigue</strong>, then a window of elevated capacity around days 3–5. Train again too early
    and you stack fatigue onto fatigue (the road to overtraining); too late and the adaptation
    has faded; just right and each wave starts higher than the last.
  </p>

  <h2>What the body adapts to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Heart and lungs</strong> to endurance work: stroke volume and VO₂max improve — the delivery system grows.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscle</strong> to heavy strength work: neural recruitment first (weeks), fibre size after (months).</li>
    <li><strong>Tendons and bone</strong> to load too — but they remodel slower than muscle, which is exactly why "too much, too soon" injures the connective tissue first.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills</strong> to practice volume with quality — technique is also an adaptation, and it is trained like one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The practical rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hard/easy rhythm, not medium/medium.</strong> Stress and recovery must alternate; chronic moderate training adapts you to chronic moderate training.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep is the strongest legal supplement.</strong> Most adaptation happens during it.</li>
    <li><strong>Increase load ~10% a week,</strong> not 10% a day — the curve punishes greed first in the knees and shoulders.</li>
    <li><strong>Track the dip, not just the peak.</strong> Rising resting heart rate, poor sleep and dread are fatigue speaking — schedule the easy day.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bannister's fitness–fatigue model; Bompa, Periodization; ACSM guidelines.</p>
</TopicPage>
